1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,480
Today's mission: Don't let stiff shoulders defeat you.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:11,240
This operation features Kaede Hondo, who has long been welcomed with applause, and...

3
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:26,760
...Tenshin Hantaro, who now receives boos only from a few long-time listeners. It is a show that has become entirely wholesome. This is Hondo Landing Operation.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,240
Good evening. I'm Kaede Hondo. And I'm Tenshin Hantaro.

5
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:37,780
So...

6
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,260
My shoulders are completely stiff.

7
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,380
Don't say it like a comedy catchphrase.

8
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:50,320
You made it sound like “ripped and muscular.” Anyway, today's objective is “Don't let stiff shoulders defeat you.” Right, you said your shoulders were stiff.

9
00:00:50,320 --> 00:01:06,880
Yes. My kind of shoulder stiffness seems unusual, so I wanted to ask everyone about it. What happens? Rather than pain, a muscle somewhere inside my shoulder starts tingling and crawling.

10
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,480
Apparently most people's shoulders hurt.

11
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:11,760
Usually they feel tight or painful.

12
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:14,380
There are medicated patches that are supposed to relieve pain, aren't there?

13
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:17,020
But mine doesn't hurt. It just tingles.

14
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:21,160
So I press it firmly with my hand or lie on a massage ball...

15
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:28,320
...and roll around on it, trying somehow to fall asleep.

16
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:33,360
Is it bad enough to keep you awake? Yes, the tingling keeps me from sleeping.

17
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:41,360
It happened last night. My shoulder kept tingling, so I bought a medicated patch.

18
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,800
I put it on. Hopefully it works.

19
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,280
Hopefully.

20
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:50,120
And despite those stiff shoulders, here we are doing the show today.

21
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:51,620
So it's tingling even now?

22
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,620
If I focus on it, I notice the tingling more.

23
00:01:55,620 --> 00:02:07,720
So I'm desperately thinking about something else. If she starts noticing it, her attention will leave the show. That's why I'm concentrating on the show right now. Thank you. How is your frozen shoulder?

24
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:10,960
My frozen shoulder has improved considerably.

25
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:20,540
As viewers can see, I can raise my arm much higher now. Look—your hand is almost level with your ear.

26
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:25,220
I used to be unable to lift it beyond about ninety degrees.

27
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:26,140
How did it improve?

28
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:27,580
What do you mean, how?

29
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:29,960
Did you work hard at rehabilitation and change your routine?

30
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,540
Yes, I kept rehabilitating and attending the clinic.

31
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:33,380
That's impressive.

32
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:35,660
I also started going to the gym.

33
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:41,320
I might soon be able to return to Brazilian jiu-jitsu. You still haven't given up on it? Of course not.

34
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,700
My jiu-jitsu uniform looks so lonely at home.

35
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:48,520
Poor thing. I bought it and then abandoned it, as if saying, “What about me?”

36
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:53,460
I hope to return someday. That's great. I'll work on recovering too.

37
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:56,180
Yes, please get rid of that tingling.

38
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:00,620
This week's introduction comes from listener Rokumonmaru. Thank you.

39
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:05,680
P.S. “I never thought the day would come when booing Hantaro-san was prohibited.”

40
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:13,060
“It's a little sad, but I suppose times change.” That's right—I prohibited it on this show.

41
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,880
I will not tolerate any more of it.

42
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:21,280
Times change. What is considered good or bad...

43
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:25,460
...can change in only a year. It really can.

44
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:29,900
So this time, we would like to proceed without booing.

45
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:36,520
But one thing that never changes is how funny this show is! You startled me by shouting so suddenly.

46
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,300
You really stepped on the accelerator there.

47
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:41,280
Of course I raised my voice—we're recording a radio show.

48
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:48,560
Naturally. The show remains funny. Why did you respond with “uh...”?

49
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:52,360
You made it sound as though there are times when it becomes less funny. That's not true.

50
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,720
It is consistently funny.

51
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:55,480
“Consistently.”

52
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:59,260
We have several different segments tonight...

53
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:04,100
...so let's enjoy ourselves. Kaede Hondo and Tenshin Hantaro's Hondo Landing Operation...

54
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:05,300
...starts now.

55
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:12,240
This program is sponsored by Anime Blast.

56
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:15,340
So this is Anime Blast?

57
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:20,380
The place where we landed was the legendary continent of Anime Blast.

58
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:24,940
Here, we will discover new ways to enjoy anime.

59
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:28,840
Our dream is far from over. Let's go, Hantaro!

60
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:32,280
Search for “Anime Blast” to see what happens next. Search!

61
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:32,960
That's old-fashioned.

62
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:35,200
Are you ready?

63
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,580
I'm Kaede Hondo. I'm Tenshin Hantaro.

64
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,620
Hondo Landing Operation.

65
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:46,240
I'm Kaede Hondo. I'm Tenshin Hantaro. And now...

66
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:53,040
We've been bringing you the show, and during that last part...

67
00:04:53,040 --> 00:05:01,340
I was tapping the paper on the desk in rhythm. Then I looked over and saw our writer keeping exactly the same rhythm.

68
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:04,160
I was incredibly embarrassed. You both looked as though you were having a wonderful time.

69
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:10,600
Two-thirds of the people in the booth had started an impromptu jam session.

70
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:16,460
That's wonderful, though there isn't much else to do.

71
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:18,980
It was during the commercial break, after all.

72
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:22,740
Now, let's begin with this segment.

73
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:28,880
Hondo-san, please introduce it. Anime Blast Landing Operation!

74
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:30,860
Presented by Fujishoji: to bring people new ways of enjoying anime...

75
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:46,040
...Anime Blast was launched as a new project. In this segment, we stand on the front line promoting it. Today's theme is “My Secret Blast,” where listeners send in things they rarely admit publicly.

76
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:54,660
Some submissions strongly resonate with Hondo-san, while others simply surprise her. Let's examine them.

77
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:57,460
This one is from Kirigame-san.

78
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:58,280
Thank you.

79
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:09,720
“My Secret Blast is a longest-crying-child championship I used to hold silently in my head while working part-time. Many children visited my workplace and often cried loudly.”

80
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:23,200
“To distract myself, I timed them and narrated internally: ‘The boy in black glasses is being comforted by his parent, but three minutes have elapsed.’”

81
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:28,320
“‘The girl in yellow fails to exceed five minutes and is eliminated.’”

82
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:32,200
“I would commentate like that entirely by myself.”

83
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:39,700
“Even now, when I hear a child crying in town, I sometimes start counting: one, two, three...”

84
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:44,080
“The all-time record is eighteen minutes.”

85
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:44,760
Isn't that long?

86
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:45,340
Very long.

87
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:46,760
They'd run out of tears.

88
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:48,200
They'd be exhausted.

89
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:50,080
They'd sleep for an entire day.

90
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:53,760
At least the child would sleep soundly on the way home.

91
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:55,260
True. Eighteen minutes is incredible.

92
00:06:55,260 --> 00:07:04,020
I suppose turning it into entertainment distracts you from the stress of wondering what to do when a child won't stop crying.

93
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:08,600
That's clever. Perhaps it also improves your commentary skills.

94
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:09,340
It probably does.

95
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:14,400
But imagine Kirigame-san doing this—though I don't know how old they are.

96
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:21,260
If she began crying, he might comfort her while narrating, “And she's begun to cry!”

97
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:23,280
“What will contestant Kirigame do?”

98
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,980
That would be awful.

99
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:33,260
You absolutely shouldn't do that. I wonder what happens when someone close to them starts crying.

100
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:38,800
Perhaps they feel the urge and keep glancing at the clock.

101
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:40,080
“One minute has elapsed.”

102
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:49,100
Imagine using a stopwatch while your girlfriend sobs, saying, “You don't understand me.”

103
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:53,320
And you keep checking the time. That's terrible. Be careful.

104
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:57,340
It is an interesting angle, though.

105
00:07:57,340 --> 00:08:00,200
Let's continue. This is from Kieta Ice-san.

106
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:03,460
“My Secret Blast is...”

107
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:13,040
“...that I had been spelling Hantaro-san's name incorrectly. I always wrote 飯太郎, using 太, ‘thick.’”

108
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:18,120
“I thought that was how Hantaro-san's name was written. I'm sorry. However...”

109
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:23,800
“...surely many others make the same mistake?” You're not reflecting on it at all!

110
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:31,920
“If you counted the emails, I predict most people write 太郎 with the character meaning ‘thick.’”

111
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:33,380
I wonder if that's true.

112
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:38,440
“Hantaro-san, perhaps you should clarify again that the `ta` in your name...”

113
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:44,100
“...is 大, meaning ‘large,’ not 太, meaning ‘thick.’”

114
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:49,040
Why should I have to?

115
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:52,100
I see.

116
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:54,400
People really confuse those characters?

117
00:08:54,400 --> 00:09:02,420
Honestly, even when someone writes Tenshin 飯太郎 with the wrong 太, I don't correct them.

118
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:08,640
I don't personally say, “Sorry, my name uses 大.”

119
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:10,360
I understand that feeling.

120
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:15,500
Because I personally think of myself as the “large” one.

121
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:19,260
If I corrected every person individually, they would only say “Sorry,” and that would be the end of it.

122
00:09:19,260 --> 00:09:24,040
So I let it pass while thinking, “I understand why they made that mistake.”

123
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:26,440
Should we check today's messages?

124
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:31,780
Can we get a count? There are quite a few.

125
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:35,740
They've already started checking every occurrence of Hantaro.

126
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:36,440
Thank you.

127
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:39,300
Our writer's GPT has produced the results.

128
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,980
Then let's inspect only the spelling of Hantaro.

129
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:44,420
Let's count them.

130
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:45,980
First one.

131
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:46,760
Han...

132
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:47,740
...taro.

133
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:52,040
It uses the correct official character.

134
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:54,880
Second person: Hantaro. Correct.

135
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:55,880
Hantaro.

136
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:56,540
Correct.

137
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:57,200
Hantaro.

138
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:01,060
Correct! Excellent. Let's reveal the listener names too.

139
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:03,200
Listener Katamare used the correct 飯大郎.

140
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:16,880
Nakayama Shin-chan, Kusunda Keito, and Oja also used the correct spelling. They're all familiar names! There are more: listener Hikuracchi Tantousha used the incorrect 飯太郎...

141
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:30,880
...as did Kaho-san and Tsubasa ga Attemo Tobenai-san. What a close contest! If this were the final round, it would be a nail-biter.

142
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:34,060
Who's winning? The score is four to three.

143
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:35,880
Four to three. The correct 飯大郎 wins.

144
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:38,740
Thank goodness—it barely won.

145
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:40,160
So people really do write 飯太郎.

146
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:42,740
Yes, they really do.

147
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:48,380
Honestly, the correct spelling would not normally appear unless you added it to your predictive text dictionary.

148
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:55,420
It's easy to mistype on a phone. Even I enter it by typing “meshi-dai-ro.”

149
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:56,500
Even you do that?

150
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,000
Even I do.

151
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:04,940
Four to three—an extremely narrow victory. That was close. Can a name really come this close to losing?

152
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:08,120
Rokumonmaru, who wrote this week's opening, used...

153
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:11,500
...the correct Hantaro.

154
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:18,860
So established listeners have learned it.

155
00:11:18,860 --> 00:11:31,180
But Tsubasa ga Attemo Tobenai-san was a name we hadn't heard before. Perhaps newer or less frequent correspondents are more likely to get it wrong.

156
00:11:31,180 --> 00:11:37,580
Let's say it loudly one more time: Tenshin Hantaro's name does not use 太, “thick.”

157
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:52,500
It uses 大, “large.” I was told that using 太 would distort the sexual preferences that my new name had finally corrected. Please keep that in mind. Ah, so that's why you adopted this spelling.

158
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:57,360
Yes. I was told it would make my preferences extremely normal.

159
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:03,220
That's frightening. The idea that a name-stroke reading could alter even my sexual preferences is frightening.

160
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:06,540
True. Originally you consulted them about your career, didn't you?

161
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:10,060
Yes—it was about my stage name. But do you actually feel any difference?

162
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:11,940
Of course I don't.

163
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:13,380
So you're still distorted?

164
00:12:13,380 --> 00:12:16,540
A person doesn't suddenly feel perfectly normal thanks to a name.

165
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:19,120
Are you aware that your preferences are distorted?

166
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:22,600
I don't even know whether they are. Honestly, I don't particularly want to hear about it.

167
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:31,200
Of course not. Nothing has changed, so it is hard to tell. Perhaps he simply continues liking what he has always liked.

168
00:12:31,340 --> 00:12:34,580
Could he secretly tell only the writer what his preferences are?

169
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:36,200
May I watch the writer's reaction?

170
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:37,700
What do you mean, watch their reaction?

171
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:41,560
I don't think I want to know. That's understandable.

172
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,580
The writer moved closer.

173
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:44,180
What are you asking him?

174
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:45,300
Now you're looking forward to it.

175
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:46,800
Does this count as another Secret Blast?

176
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:47,480
A secret.

177
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:48,000
What is it?

178
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:50,320
How should I put this?

179
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:54,060
Let's just watch the reaction.

180
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:58,400
Did everyone hear that just now?

181
00:12:59,100 --> 00:12:59,920
You didn't hear it?

182
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:01,820
I heard it very clearly.

183
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:02,760
Well, what was it?

184
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:06,960
Doing something censored to someone's body.

185
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:07,820
Stop that.

186
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:09,760
You obviously heard it!

187
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:12,920
Apparently, it means doing something censored to someone's body.

188
00:13:12,920 --> 00:13:18,740
Censoring it makes it sound far worse. People imagine something even more terrible.

189
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:21,440
Can I say it? Go ahead. Drawing on someone's body.

190
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:22,640
And then they say, “Hey, stop that.”

191
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:26,000
That was surprisingly cute. Maybe drawing on them while they sleep?

192
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,600
Something like that—perhaps writing `肉` or drawing hearts.

193
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:37,020
Wonderful, thank you. That sounds like a happy world. Apparently not? What is it really? This one is a secret to take to the grave.

194
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:40,000
What sort of thing? Take it to the grave, everyone.

195
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,460
Wait, it only said “body”; it didn't specifically say “face.” Never mind—many possibilities.

196
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:45,780
Let's continue.

197
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:49,880
One more message. Let's reset and approach it neutrally.

198
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:55,480
The atmosphere has become chaotic. Sorry—my name caused this.

199
00:13:55,480 --> 00:13:59,980
This is from Blasters-san: “Hondo-san, Seitaro-san...”

200
00:13:59,980 --> 00:14:04,440
They got it wrong! That's impressive.

201
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:08,100
And the `sei` in Seitaro is 聖, meaning “holy.”

202
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:09,580
They turned you into “Saint Taro.” Every part is wrong.

203
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:10,460
Everything is wrong.

204
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:11,080
Impressive.

205
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:14,480
“Something I've secretly been experimenting with lately is...”

206
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:20,100
“...ways of modifying convenience-store rice balls.” Nice. “I usually choose among the same three varieties...” That's surprisingly proper.

207
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:23,100
Then perhaps they genuinely misspelled your name. It was completely wrong.

208
00:14:23,100 --> 00:14:37,680
“Because I became bored with the same three choices, I started buying particular kinds of nori and wrapping the rice balls myself, or adding a little soy sauce.”

209
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:42,620
“Even a small variation is better than none.” That's an exceptionally wholesome submission. What did you expect?

210
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:47,680
“Do either of you ever get bored of eating the same thing and start changing its flavor yourself?”

211
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:49,760
Thank you. Hmm...

212
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:53,800
If I wanted to change a food's flavor, I would rather eat something else.

213
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:56,300
True—you would have to keep buying the same thing first.

214
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:01,420
Exactly. I don't really relate either, because I can eat the same thing repeatedly.

215
00:15:01,420 --> 00:15:04,220
For example, some people transform leftover curry...

216
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:07,600
...into curry udon on the second night.

217
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:10,560
But I am happy eating curry again unchanged.

218
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:13,600
I'm the type who needs no variation.

219
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:26,400
Still, I admire the ingenuity. Perhaps this person has few opportunities to eat anything except convenience-store food.

220
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:32,140
Maybe they eat between jobs. Many people are extremely busy.

221
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:34,820
So this is the effort they make.

222
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:39,500
Exactly. When you arrive home exhausted, you settle for the convenience store.

223
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:47,180
I understand. In that situation, perhaps adding a little soy sauce makes sense.

224
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:50,600
I was so distracted by “Seitaro-san” that I couldn't absorb the message.

225
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:55,780
Come on! You stumbled at the very beginning. Sorry, Blasters-san.

226
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:58,640
It threw us off because I changed my name.

227
00:15:58,640 --> 00:15:59,600
I think that covers it.

228
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:04,360
Some of those secrets went a little too far...

229
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:08,540
...but now let's choose tonight's winner.

230
00:16:08,540 --> 00:16:22,180
Hondo-san? I've decided: “Listener Seitaro-san—drawing on someone's body.” Wait, that's me!

231
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:24,160
Sorry. Congratulations.

232
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:27,200
So the winner is listener Seitaro-san...

233
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:40,960
...which means I receive the program's original QUO card. This is my first one! You sacrificed yourself for the segment, so we'll send it with our gratitude.

234
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:45,740
And with that, this segment reaches its final episode today.

235
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:49,640
Is that all right? Was this really the right ending? Is it okay for me to be the final winner?

236
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:54,420
Perhaps we should instead choose the person whose message led us here...

237
00:16:54,420 --> 00:16:55,900
Kieta Ice-san.

238
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:57,660
Kieta Ice-san.

239
00:16:57,660 --> 00:16:58,900
We'll choose them.

240
00:16:58,900 --> 00:16:59,780
Yes, let's do that. That was close.

241
00:16:59,780 --> 00:17:00,540
Thank you.

242
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:03,060
Otherwise people would ask why I won the final installment.

243
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:03,680
That was close.

244
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:04,780
Very close.

245
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:07,840
As mentioned, this was the final installment.

246
00:17:07,840 --> 00:17:15,080
Thank you for submitting to and participating in Anime Blast Landing Operation.

247
00:17:15,080 --> 00:17:17,500
Anime Blast Information Bureau is currently available on Anime Blast's official YouTube channel.

248
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:22,020
Their official X account also runs various campaigns.

249
00:17:22,020 --> 00:17:30,120
Please follow and check the official Anime Blast account. There is also a new project derived from Anime Blast...

250
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:36,800
...Blast V, which is now fully underway. The VTuber project Blast V follows the concept “Enjoy every aspect of Japanese culture.”

251
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:45,840
It uses VTubers to introduce Japan's culture, traditions, and contemporary entertainment to the world, primarily through YouTube streams.

252
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:52,820
On August 26, three first-generation Blast V VTubers made their streaming debuts.

253
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:57,720
For details about their future activities...

254
00:17:57,720 --> 00:18:00,020
...check Blast V's official X account.

255
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:04,220
That concludes tonight's Anime Blast Landing Operation.

256
00:18:04,220 --> 00:18:10,340
Time to go home! Hello, everyone! Thank you so much!

257
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:13,060
Yay!

258
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:14,120
Bye!

259
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:18,460
I went to New York.

260
00:18:18,460 --> 00:18:23,100
New York Yankees.

261
00:18:23,100 --> 00:18:23,900
The cap, you mean.

262
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:26,640
Our video viewers can see it now.

263
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:34,880
Your cap really is a New York Yankees cap. So you went to New York. Annyeong...

264
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,560
Is it “haseyo”?

265
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:39,280
Annyeong.

266
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:47,860
I appeared at an event in New York for the television anime The 100 Girlfriends Who Really, Really, Really, Really, Really Love You.

267
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:54,300
You appeared on stage at the event. Anime really does take you around the world.

268
00:18:54,300 --> 00:19:09,960
Wataru Kato, Miyu Tomita, and I went together. Since we are close, I felt relaxed and enjoyed it without much nervousness. I had traveled abroad before the COVID pandemic, but...

269
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:16,880
...this was my first trip abroad since then. So your first in about six years? Yes, I hadn't gone abroad for roughly six years.

270
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:19,680
It was incredibly fun, and my first visit to New York.

271
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:22,280
How many hours is the flight to New York?

272
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:23,800
Around thirteen or fourteen hours.

273
00:19:23,800 --> 00:19:24,960
That's a long flight.

274
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:27,840
I could barely sleep on the way there.

275
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:30,140
But I slept soundly on the way home.

276
00:19:30,140 --> 00:19:36,100
What did you do on the outbound flight? Normally there is a monitor...

277
00:19:36,100 --> 00:19:37,980
...on the seat in front of you.

278
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:44,700
I planned to watch it, but my seat had no monitor in front—only a wall, like a bulkhead.

279
00:19:44,700 --> 00:19:46,380
Airplanes have seats like that?

280
00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:49,280
Yes, it was a seat at the front of a section.

281
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:50,280
Oh, I understand.

282
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:52,880
There was a restroom ahead of me rather than another seat.

283
00:19:52,880 --> 00:19:55,940
So there truly was no seat in front of you.

284
00:19:55,940 --> 00:20:00,320
Thirteen hours with no screen—what was I supposed to do?

285
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:01,440
What can you do?

286
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:03,420
I hadn't downloaded anything to my phone.

287
00:20:03,420 --> 00:20:04,800
Because you expected to use the monitor.

288
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:08,640
I tried to sleep until the first in-flight meal arrived.

289
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:18,720
I couldn't sleep, ate the meal, tried sleeping again, dozed briefly, ate again, and repeated the cycle. It was rough.

290
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:32,940
Another passenger in the same kind of front-row seat apparently had a monitor stored inside the armrest. So there actually was one.

291
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:36,740
I noticed partway through and tried to take mine out, but couldn't.

292
00:20:36,740 --> 00:20:39,420
Couldn't you have asked someone?

293
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:43,180
In hindsight, yes, but I was already exhausted.

294
00:20:43,180 --> 00:20:57,680
I gave up and tried hard to sleep instead. An older gentleman I didn't know was sitting beside me. I also couldn't work out how to recline my seat.

295
00:20:57,680 --> 00:21:11,720
As I experimented, the gentleman gestured and showed me which button to use. I replied, “Thank you.”

296
00:21:11,720 --> 00:21:20,400
We exchanged a “You're welcome,” and I thought he was a kind foreign gentleman. Then I noticed his phone interface was entirely in Japanese. I could simply have said `ありがとう`.

297
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:23,060
Oh, he was Japanese.

298
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:26,900
You must each have assumed the other spoke a different language.

299
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:34,980
For no reason, both of you obscured your Japanese and communicated in vague English: “Yes, thank you.”

300
00:21:34,980 --> 00:21:37,960
Then his phone displayed a lot of Japanese...

301
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:42,440
...because he was using a coloring-book app. He spent the whole flight coloring.

302
00:21:42,440 --> 00:21:43,640
Coloring sounds nice.

303
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:46,040
I looked at it thinking, “The app is in Japanese.”

304
00:21:46,040 --> 00:21:47,000
Then it dawned on me.

305
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:51,020
So I made the trip with help from kind people.

306
00:21:51,020 --> 00:21:53,680
How many nights did you stay?

307
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:57,080
Three nights there. Three nights?

308
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:00,260
It was a tightly packed trip.

309
00:22:00,260 --> 00:22:00,700
I see.

310
00:22:00,700 --> 00:22:03,020
First, we arrived...

311
00:22:03,020 --> 00:22:04,620
...in the evening.

312
00:22:04,620 --> 00:22:06,920
New York greeted us with rain.

313
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:08,620
It looked incredibly cool.

314
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:11,940
You fly to New York, naturally...

315
00:22:11,940 --> 00:22:21,100
...but shortly after takeoff, the plane became extremely cold. Was America simply going to be cold?

316
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:24,940
It was as if the powerful air-conditioning was America's opening jab.

317
00:22:24,940 --> 00:22:28,100
I had brought a long-sleeved zip-up hoodie.

318
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:36,360
I put it on but was still cold. Even after wrapping myself in the supplied blanket, I was cold.

319
00:22:36,360 --> 00:22:49,980
That cold? When we arrived, New York was rainy but not particularly humid. Everyone had arranged for us to go out to eat, and they took us to a wonderful steakhouse.

320
00:22:49,980 --> 00:22:52,660
That sounds perfect—but you were still freezing?

321
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:55,560
It was incredibly cold. Were you all right?

322
00:22:55,700 --> 00:22:58,700
Was everyone shivering?

323
00:22:58,700 --> 00:23:03,740
Everyone was trembling. So I tried to warm myself with alcohol...

324
00:23:03,740 --> 00:23:12,520
...while some people drank hot tea. The air conditioning was blasting everywhere in New York.

325
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:15,000
Everyone should remember that. I certainly will.

326
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:15,780
At this time of year...

327
00:23:15,780 --> 00:23:17,860
At this time of year? I see.

328
00:23:17,860 --> 00:23:23,720
I suppose they prefer making places too cold. It felt like complimentary air conditioning.

329
00:23:23,720 --> 00:23:28,620
But the event was fun. Was it the following day?

330
00:23:28,620 --> 00:23:32,020
Yes, the first event was the next day.

331
00:23:32,020 --> 00:23:36,060
There was an autograph session and a stage appearance.

332
00:23:36,060 --> 00:23:46,960
The following day had another autograph session plus the largest stage event in the evening. One thing that always surprises me about overseas audiences is...

333
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:53,100
...that they react before the interpreter speaks. Some people learn Japanese through anime.

334
00:23:53,100 --> 00:23:57,760
People often say that. Learning Japanese from 100 Girlfriends could have alarming consequences.

335
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:01,460
You probably shouldn't; you would end up choosing very strange vocabulary.

336
00:24:01,460 --> 00:24:05,140
It isn't the anime you should study from! But it is a beloved anime.

337
00:24:05,140 --> 00:24:08,720
They love it, so it can't be helped. In any case, the event was tremendous fun.

338
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:11,780
People always say overseas audiences react enthusiastically.

339
00:24:11,780 --> 00:24:16,580
Perhaps it is a cultural difference.

340
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:23,740
They shouted things like “Woo!” and “I love you!” It was amazing to hear.

341
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:26,240
I couldn't understand the exact words...

342
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:27,380
...because I don't understand English...

343
00:24:27,380 --> 00:24:30,060
...but I could feel their enthusiasm and thought it was incredible.

344
00:24:30,060 --> 00:24:32,460
It gave me so much energy.

345
00:24:32,460 --> 00:24:35,040
The audience and the event energized you.

346
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:35,640
They did.

347
00:24:35,640 --> 00:24:36,920
And the food was delicious.

348
00:24:36,920 --> 00:24:38,760
It was. I ate a tremendous amount.

349
00:24:38,760 --> 00:24:41,060
Did you do any sightseeing?

350
00:24:41,060 --> 00:24:42,700
Yes.

351
00:24:42,700 --> 00:24:50,780
I went to MoMA—the Museum of Modern Art.

352
00:24:50,780 --> 00:24:59,080
We decided to walk there while it was still light and sightsee along the way.

353
00:24:59,080 --> 00:25:01,940
But on the way...

354
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:15,080
...we started wondering whether American McDonald's tasted different. We agreed to stop if we saw one—and then we did, so our group of about ten went inside.

355
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:21,780
The entire 100 Girlfriends team ordered food together. It tasted about the same, but it was good.

356
00:25:21,780 --> 00:25:26,100
We relaxed there until we realized we had to keep walking.

357
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:29,560
Then we reached MoMA.

358
00:25:29,560 --> 00:25:34,740
By then, we only had enough time to look around for thirty or forty minutes.

359
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:34,940
What?

360
00:25:35,660 --> 00:25:36,080
That was all?

361
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:39,540
We had spent too long enjoying McDonald's.

362
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:45,180
We ran out of time; when forced to choose between MoMA and McDonald's, we effectively chose McDonald's.

363
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:48,620
I don't regret it, though. The local staff member and interpreter...

364
00:25:48,620 --> 00:25:54,040
...offered to obtain the tickets for us. Thank you.

365
00:25:54,040 --> 00:26:00,220
While they did that, we decided to look at the gift shop first.

366
00:26:00,220 --> 00:26:03,120
I excitedly bought things for myself.

367
00:26:03,120 --> 00:26:07,940
Sorry—I forgot to buy souvenirs. I didn't expect that confession in the middle of this story.

368
00:26:07,940 --> 00:26:09,700
I only bought things for myself and Ami-chan.

369
00:26:09,700 --> 00:26:17,840
So only your absolute inner circle received souvenirs. I forgot my parents and everyone at work too.

370
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:19,760
Souvenirs become luggage, and you had little time.

371
00:26:19,760 --> 00:26:28,800
My English-speaking manager and I started on the top floor of MoMA, saw everything in about twenty minutes, and left.

372
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:31,040
That is extremely short.

373
00:26:31,040 --> 00:26:34,340
After that, what was it—not “Brand New Day”...

374
00:26:34,340 --> 00:26:35,820
Broadway.

375
00:26:35,820 --> 00:26:37,120
Oh, Broadway.

376
00:26:37,120 --> 00:26:40,580
I don't know much about it, but we saw a Broadway musical.

377
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:43,460
Wataru Kato had said he wanted to see one...

378
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:46,620
...so they made it happen. They said, “Sure, let's go.”

379
00:26:46,620 --> 00:26:53,900
I joined them, but I was so exhausted that I fell asleep and woke up at the climax. Then I enjoyed it.

380
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:56,380
That doesn't count as enjoying it!

381
00:26:56,380 --> 00:26:59,000
Of course I hadn't intended to fall asleep.

382
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:00,980
The women were tremendous singers and looked incredibly cool.

383
00:27:00,980 --> 00:27:06,380
It was the musical SIX, a story about six wives—former wives.

384
00:27:06,380 --> 00:27:11,700
I think it has also been performed here. It was extremely entertaining, though I couldn't understand the English.

385
00:27:11,700 --> 00:27:14,680
It was amazing. Thank you. Goodbye.

386
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:18,560
Goodbye to New York. It was an enjoyable trip.

387
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:22,660
Now for the ending and announcements.

388
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:24,800
I appear in various works.

389
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:42,760
[Several work titles are unclear in both source transcripts.] On September 21, Hantaro will hold a staged-reading event at Yoshimoto Makuhari Aeon Mall Theater.

390
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:57,400
Shuri Miyumi and Saho Shirasu will perform a staged reading and appear in a separate talk event. There will be two performances. Hantaro has also written original material, so please come.

391
00:27:57,400 --> 00:28:01,140
Thank you. Oh, we're finished.

392
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:12,280
Your hosts were Kaede Hondo saying, “Goodbye, good night,” and Tenshin Hantaro saying, “Thank you.” This mission of Hondo Landing Operation is now complete.
